[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not find any evidence that a “big router” is down or that the other campus has disappeared. Core routing via OSPF is healthy end‑to‑end. On router17 (the central interconnect for the remote campus), FRR is running and OSPF adjacencies are Full with all peers: 55.5.1.2 (sshswitch), 10.1.0.1 (router11), 192.168.144.1 (router10), and 172.29.10.1 (router8). Its routing table contains all campus prefixes (e.g., 10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24, 10.200.1.0/24 via 55.5.1.2; and 10.0.0.0/24, 10.1.x/24, 192.168.144.0/24 via the correct neighbors).

From user LANs, inter‑campus reachability works: from a host in VLAN10 (pc5, 172.16.10.5) I can ping router7 (192.168.10.1), router16 (10.2.2.1), router17 (55.5.1.1), router11 (10.1.0.1), router10 (192.168.144.1), and router8 (172.29.10.1) with 0% loss. Similarly, from a VLAN20 host (pc10) I can reach router7, router17, and router16 cleanly. Topology‑wide host reachability across the 10.5/10.6/172.16.x networks is also 100% in our matrix probes.

Mechanistically, if the “big router” were actually down, we would see OSPF neighbors go down and corresponding routes drop, and pings to the remote routers would fail from user subnets. None of that is occurring. Localization therefore indicates no failing interface or daemon on router17, router7, router10, router11, or sshswitch (FRR active on each where applicable).

Root cause: no ongoing network fault detected; likely a transient event or a non-network issue (e.g., DNS/application).

Corrective fix: no action required. If users still see problems, please provide specific IPs/subnets; we will trace path and DNS at that time.
=== END ===